 enough  counterintuitive so that way when you  think about how to deal with the  information you're presented if you  aren't careful you can actually end up  making poor decisions or at least not  the right decision in terms of being  able to process that information so if  some of you are familiar with the work  in decision theory or psychology that  people have talked about regarding bias  and these decision issues you may have  seen one or two of the things I'm going  to talk about but I am also going to  talk about some things from experimental  philosophy and from work by persi  diaconis who's a mathematician who  studied coincidences so I hope that  there will be something new here for  absolutely everyone okay now having  thought about what factors make good  decisions let me now turn to the next  item in the poll which is what can get  in the way of making a good decisions  okay here we see responses muddled  thinking emotions in various variables  you haven't tried to control for an  arrow mindset bias seeds and so on fear  clarity okay these are all very good  points and I'm going to come back to  some of these themes as well  particularly clarity because not being  clear about what is the precise question  that's being asked can often lead to  poor decisions okay so with that now let  me turn to the  so let me turn to the next thing the  next aspect of the talk so this is what  I want to cover so I want to begin with  a very brief introduction on decisions  and then look at some areas where our  standard intuitions can go awry and  these will break down into five  different areas and then I want to just  close with some remarks about where you  can go for further reading or further  information on decision making if you're  interested okay so to begin with so I  think one of the things that's really  fascinating about making decisions and  how people have studied making decisions  is that effectively since the very  beginnings of philosophy in the Natural  Sciences the standard conception of  human decision makers has been this  highly idealized perfectly rational  decision maker and so right there I've  got a photograph of the bronze bust of  Minerva the Roman goddess of wisdom art  and commerce and it's this highly  idealized rational agent that's always  been seen as the core of how we think  about decision-making but through the  work of Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky  and a whole host of other people we now  know that there are a whole host of  different kinds of biases and aspects to  our thinking which can get in the way of  how we process information and can  actually lead us astray and how we make  decisions you know both in terms of  conscious bias and statistical bias and  also ways in which our brains can kind  of leap to conclusions  in ways that we are not always aware of  what I want to do now is actually going  to go through and explore some of these  ok so to begin base rate basics let me  begin with a story or just a kind of a  thought experiment  so think about medical testing right  this is going to be a just a kind of a  case study but once I talk through this  hopefully you can see how it'll easily  generalize to a lot of other cases that  you'll be familiar with ok so suppose  that were you're going to the doctor and  you need to take a medical test that is  pretty accurate but of course you know  no test is perfectly accurate  all of the time right so suppose this  medical test for some condition will  mistakenly identify a healthy person as  having the disease 5% of the time but  let's suppose that if this tested least  has the benefit that it will never fail  to identify an unhealthy person okay so  if that's the case now suppose that  we've got a group of 10,000 people and  what do you know about that 10,000  people is that 2 percent of them have  the disease okay so suppose this is just  part of your background knowledge okay  now you go to your doctor they do the  test and then you find out that you get  a positive result now the question I  want to ask is what is the probability  of having the disease given a positive  test result okay so now you can just  think about that for a moment now what's  the probability of having the disease  given a positive test result just think  I mean think about this  okay so what are some people saying so  some people say ninety five percent five  percent two percent two out of seven  okay so what else do people think okay  so depends on the pretest probability  okay it's a good point so in the terms  of the pretest probability you know that  two percent of the population has the  disease the test never incorrectly  identify someone with the disease but it  does incorrectly identify healthy people  five percent of the time that's all the  information you need to know okay  fantastic  so this I think nicely highlights the  thing that can the point about base  rates I think many people have said 90%  of the time ninety-five percent of the  time and so on so I think you might be  surprised okay so this graphic over here  on the right so it's a bunch of dots  don't bother counting them I can promise  you that there are 10,000 dots there one  dot for each person in the population  okay now what we know is that two  percent of the population have the  disease so it's two hundred people we  know that two percent have that and we  know that the test is never wrong so the  test will always identify those people  as having the disease that's where this  little green to rose at the bottom is  about those are the people that the test  gives a positive result for but we know  that everyone who doesn't have the  disease so that is all of the other grey  dots here are healthy but we know that  the test is wrong five percent of the  time for those people so five percent of  those people will get the positive test  result so those are the red dots down  there  so when you say what's the chance that  you've got the disease given a positive  test result what you have to look about  is well how many people is the positive  result correct four versus the total  population who get the positive test  result and that's two hundred divided by  two  200 plus 490 29% so the thing that's  important about this is that in cases  where the base rate the 2% of the  population is very low your intuitions  about what the meaning of a positive  test is can be really quite misleading  [Applause]  you

